A||N||9||bHooper||Rear main oil seal removal question||||5||
Z||000000||bHooper||04-08-2000||03:24 PM||||Does anyone have a good dip for removing and reinstalling the rear main oil seal?<P>Since I am putting in my new tranny, I thought I would go ahead and do this.<P>thanks,<BR>hoop||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||04-08-2000||05:02 PM||||acording to the books, getting it out is easy. getting the new one in requires special tools or you risk trashing the new one.<P>You could make something. it's mostly a matter of finding tubing the size and thickness needed so the seal will go in straight w/o getting cut.||||reg||1
Z||000002||bHooper||04-09-2000||12:11 AM||||actually it looks the other way... to me... i dont want to booger up anything removeing the seal.  i would think that putting it in would be easier if you are carefull||||reg||1
Z||000003||Zman302||04-09-2000||12:39 AM||||Its not really that hard of a job.  I used a scribe and slowly wiggled it back and forth untill it started to turn and then dug into it and pryed it out.  installing the new seal is easy too, just take some fresh motor oil and smear around the seal and push back in. then slowly tap around the outside of the seal with a flat punch untill the seal is seated.||||reg||1
Z||000004||bHooper||04-09-2000||11:40 AM||||define "scribe tool" please... i have a guess but wouild like to be sure.<P>thanks<BR>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000005||bHooper||04-09-2000||03:45 PM||||Success!  I managed to remove the old seal.  I used one small screw driver to wedge between the crank and seal, prying outwards, and a smaller one between the block and sealuntil i had enough of it out that i could use the needle nose pliers to remove it.  Actually very simple , once you get over the initial fear!<P>Now, the new seal, that I purchase from NAPA, looks entirely different from the old one.  It is a one piece rubber with a visible spring inside, the part that faces towards the crank shaft is "open", unlike the previouse "sealed" unit with a felt dust cover.<P>Any thoughts on that one?  Is it ok to use the NAPA one?  or should I repalce it with a duplicate of the old one?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Zman302||04-09-2000||10:07 PM||||the seal from napa will work just fine...i have used seals like that before and have not had any trouble with them.  good luck with your car.   Z||||reg||1
Z||000007||bHooper||04-10-2000||08:21 PM||||Ok, so I've replaced this thing... is there a way to test it out (while I have the engine and tranny out)?  I would hate to reasemble everything and have it gush oil all over the place.<P>Would it help if I put a quart or two of oil in the engine, let it settle and look for any leaks?||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||04-11-2000||12:07 PM||||unless you flood the oil pan that won't tell you any thing. Even then you'll likely only know with it running.<P>as long as you were carefull and made sure the crank surface was in good shape the worse thing you'll get is a drip if there is still a problem you missed... This is a non presurized area, it won't gush.||||reg||1
Z||000009||bHooper||04-11-2000||01:12 PM||||so, really, the only way to know for sure is to reassemble everything?  i was carefull, the crank and block surfaces were clean, i lubed the seal and tamped it in until it met the inner "lip" and wouldnt sit any further.<P>thanks again, Ogre!<P>hoop||||reg||1
